  "text": "**Solution 1 is wrong.** For t = 2 its answer is ρ = 1. A graph with ρ = 1 is a matching plus isolated vertices, which contains no P₄. Adding one edge creates at most one P₄, so it cannot reach 2P₄.\n\n**Solution 2 is wrong.** Its extremal graph K₃₍ₜ₋₁₎ ∪ K_{n−3(t−1)} is not tP₄-free in general. For t = 2 and n = 20, K₁₇ alone contains four disjoint P₄'s. Its formula also grows linearly in n. The join K₃ ∨ K̄_{n−3}, which is tP₄-saturated for n ≥ 8, has ρ = 1 + √(3n − 8), which is far smaller for large n. The n = 14 row in its table is also inconsistent: the graph listed there has three components, not two, so the formula does not apply to it.\n\n**Solution 3 is wrong.** Its join K_{2t−1} ∨ K̄_{n−2t+1} is tP₄-free and saturated, and its quotient-matrix computation is correct. But it is not the minimum. Take t = 2, n = 8 and G = K₃ ∪ K₅:\n- G has no 2P₄, since the K₃ component cannot host a P₄ and K₅ holds only one.\n- Adding any cross edge av gives the P₄ b–c–a–v, and the remaining K₄ on the other K₅ vertices gives a second P₄. So G is 2P₄-saturated.\n- ρ(G) = 4, while the join gives 1 + √13 ≈ 4.6, not 5. Solution 3's formula is therefore not the minimum at (2, 8).\n\nNone of the three final answers is the correct minimum. I did not establish the true value, so I cannot name the correct formula.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
